WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.145 --> 00:00:13.582
Good afternoon, so today we're
going to do something different.

00:00:13.582 --> 00:00:17.551
One of the things that we've learned at
Y Combinator, is that one of the best ways

00:00:17.551 --> 00:00:20.707
to learn about startups is to
watch other startups get advice.

00:00:20.707 --> 00:00:24.325
And so we're going to do live office hours
which we've done a few times before,

00:00:24.325 --> 00:00:26.002
in this class, three of four times.

00:00:26.002 --> 00:00:28.220
We'll try to do three companies each time.

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:31.860
This is Yuri, also works at Y Combinator
and going to do this with me.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.470
And we're going to just give
advice to three startups.

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:36.370
You have to be very brave to do this.

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:38.880
So I'm very thankful for
the startups that have agreed to do this.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.710
These are some of the people that
are taking the course online.

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:42.414
And we'll just get going.

00:00:46.838 --> 00:00:49.357
So do you want to tell us, well first
do you want to introduce yourselves.

00:00:49.357 --> 00:00:50.564
Does this work?

00:00:50.564 --> 00:00:53.362
So I'm Sarah and
this is my co-founder Andrew.

00:00:53.362 --> 00:00:55.900
We're building Canny.

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.726
Canny helps teams collect and
manage feedback from their users.

00:00:58.726 --> 00:01:04.158
Essentially, what it is is teams can
integrate our product into their

00:01:04.158 --> 00:01:09.420
website or mobile app and
that allows their users to post feedback.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:10.337
&gt;&gt; Is it live?

00:01:10.337 --> 00:01:11.277
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:01:11.277 --> 00:01:13.691
&gt;&gt; How many websites have integrated?

00:01:13.691 --> 00:01:17.058
&gt;&gt; We launched our a month
ago on Product Hunt.

00:01:17.058 --> 00:01:19.380
That was pretty good traffic.

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:23.536
We have 25 paying customers.

00:01:23.536 --> 00:01:24.557
So those are people,

00:01:24.557 --> 00:01:27.402
who have either integrated or
are using us in a certain way.

00:01:27.402 --> 00:01:33.065
But yeah, we got maybe around 500
companies from the project launch.

00:01:33.065 --> 00:01:34.042
&gt;&gt; That's great.
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:01:34.042 --> 00:01:35.423
&gt;&gt; How much are you charging?

00:01:35.423 --> 00:01:36.770
&gt;&gt; It's a range.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:43.511
So on the low end we have a $2 plan
which was our alternative to freemium.

00:01:43.511 --> 00:01:49.775
GPM [LAUGH] and it goes from 19.49,
129 from there.

00:01:49.775 --> 00:01:52.635
It's really early and we're trying to
iterate on this and figure out what our

00:01:52.635 --> 00:01:56.014
best price point is which is probably one
of our main challenges right now I think.

00:01:56.014 --> 00:01:56.761
&gt;&gt; Go ahead.

00:01:56.761 --> 00:01:57.273
No, no.

00:01:57.273 --> 00:01:59.571
&gt;&gt; How did even arrive at
the first set of numbers to test?

00:01:59.571 --> 00:02:02.323
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so
I think the $2 one was kind of special.

00:02:02.323 --> 00:02:05.191
We were flip-flopping between Freemium and
and Cheapium.

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:07.790
And I think that was
a good decision because

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:10.903
it narrowed down our
potential customers by a lot.

00:02:10.903 --> 00:02:13.954
And we figured that people who
were not going to pay us anything

00:02:13.954 --> 00:02:15.732
were never going to pay us anything.

00:02:15.732 --> 00:02:19.494
&gt;&gt; How many of the 25
customers are on the $2 plan?

00:02:19.494 --> 00:02:21.433
&gt;&gt; Maybe,
&gt;&gt; Seven.

00:02:21.433 --> 00:02:21.956
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:02:21.956 --> 00:02:22.861
&gt;&gt; Really, that's great.

00:02:22.861 --> 00:02:25.692
And what's the other most popular plan,
is it 19?

00:02:25.692 --> 00:02:28.572
&gt;&gt; 19,
we limited our $2 plan very heavily.

00:02:28.572 --> 00:02:33.100
So it's really people who are a solo
founder, probably no revenue.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:36.072
&gt;&gt; What do you get with the pricing?

00:02:36.072 --> 00:02:38.778
I think I looked at your website,
it's how many users do you have?

00:02:38.778 --> 00:02:41.332
&gt;&gt; Right, so right now we're charging
by how many end users you have.

00:02:41.332 --> 00:02:45.238
That was kind of our proxy to how much
value the company was going to get from

00:02:45.238 --> 00:02:45.999
our product.

00:02:45.999 --> 00:02:50.813
This is kind of a challenge, especially
because we can't particularly tell

00:02:50.813 --> 00:02:54.750
how many users they have, so
we're iterating on this as well.

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:56.540
&gt;&gt; What do people really love about this?

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:57.970
Why do they use your product and

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:00.730
not one of the many other services
to accomplish the same goal?

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:06.035
Yeah, I mean there's a lot of different
values that you get out of posting and

00:03:06.035 --> 00:03:07.033
voting on features.

00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:10.617
One of them is you have there's
a lot of transparencies so

00:03:10.617 --> 00:03:12.640
you keep your users in the loop.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:15.098
A lot of current feedback
solutions is email or

00:03:15.098 --> 00:03:17.429
live chat where the user sends an email,
and

00:03:17.429 --> 00:03:21.981
then the company says, we'll pass it on
to the team, is what inevitably happens.

00:03:21.981 --> 00:03:25.615
And then the user doesn't really know,
like did my voice get heard?

00:03:25.615 --> 00:03:29.400
And so we've heard both from teams and
users that this is a great experience.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:32.047
Their users are happy, they know what
feedback their users are giving.

00:03:32.047 --> 00:03:33.546
Versus email and live chat,

00:03:33.546 --> 00:03:37.051
which takes just a ton of time to
process all these conversations.

00:03:37.051 --> 00:03:39.214
&gt;&gt; Of the people that are using it,

00:03:39.214 --> 00:03:44.276
what's the average number of questions
that have been posted on it so far?

00:03:44.276 --> 00:03:46.819
&gt;&gt; Average number of questions,
what do you mean by that?

00:03:46.819 --> 00:03:49.280
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so people put request or
something else that they're voting on.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:54.947
Do you know of the 25 paying customers how
many on average have had questions posted?

00:03:54.947 --> 00:03:58.644
&gt;&gt; Well it really depends on how
many end users they have, and

00:03:58.644 --> 00:04:01.669
also how well they've
implemented our tools.

00:04:01.669 --> 00:04:05.072
Like if it's less discoverable
they're going to less feedback and so

00:04:05.072 --> 00:04:08.182
we've been trying to help that
by giving them design advice.

00:04:08.182 --> 00:04:14.405
But like our top team
probably has 300 posts.

00:04:14.405 --> 00:04:18.712
And the thing about this versus
email is that you might get.

00:04:18.712 --> 00:04:22.752
If a post has 100 votes you
might get 100 different emails.

00:04:22.752 --> 00:04:27.185
And so we help you save time by responding
to all those 100 people at once,

00:04:27.185 --> 00:04:30.393
instead of sending individual
emails back and forth.

00:04:30.393 --> 00:04:33.273
&gt;&gt; Do you feel like you've gotten to
the point where you have a product that

00:04:33.273 --> 00:04:34.329
some people really love?

00:04:34.329 --> 00:04:38.949
And at this point, if so, I guess,
do you think you're at that point first?

00:04:38.949 --> 00:04:42.349
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I feel like we have a sliver
of product market fit where some people

00:04:42.349 --> 00:04:44.209
are extremely happy with our product.

00:04:44.209 --> 00:04:48.633
And we kind of know the next steps we need
to do to expand that by ten x to address

00:04:48.633 --> 00:04:50.513
a larger portion of the market.

00:04:50.513 --> 00:04:51.234
&gt;&gt; What are the steps?

00:04:51.234 --> 00:04:55.875
&gt;&gt; Integrations with other
SaaS services like Intercom,

00:04:55.875 --> 00:05:00.324
Slack, Zapier, and
then a better answer on mobile.

00:05:00.324 --> 00:05:04.019
Is the biggest challenge you face now
just trying to figure out how to grow and

00:05:04.019 --> 00:05:05.360
scale this, do you think?

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:07.016
Or is there still a lot of product work,

00:05:07.016 --> 00:05:09.943
other than sort of like integrations
that are meant to drive growth?

00:05:09.943 --> 00:05:12.845
Is there still a lot of product
work before you want to scale?

00:05:12.845 --> 00:05:14.750
&gt;&gt; There's some product work, for sure.

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:18.508
I think the core product is pretty good,
but it takes a while for our leads to get

00:05:18.508 --> 00:05:21.840
stuck on our product, because they
have to integrate into their product.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:23.280
So it's a pretty big investment.

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:24.660
&gt;&gt; How easy is it to integrate it?

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:25.690
&gt;&gt; It's pretty easy.

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:26.560
It's really easy.

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:30.430
It takes half an hour, to an hour, is
what we see developers spending time on.

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:33.700
But it's just I think we need better
onboarding to convince them that this is

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:34.800
worth doing.

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:36.770
And so I think right now
our funnel is pretty leaky,

00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:38.560
so that's what we're onboarding.

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:39.580
&gt;&gt; What's the biggest value of that?

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:43.560
So your biggest user has 300 questions or
a couple hundred questions?

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:44.185
&gt;&gt; Several hundred.

00:05:44.185 --> 00:05:45.630
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Right, and so

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:47.380
what's the biggest value
that they see out of it?

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:48.140
What do they tell you.

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:52.420
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's hard because our
value add is very diverse.

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:54.050
Like one thing is customer satisfaction.

00:05:54.050 --> 00:05:56.990
Another thing is product management,
what you want to work on.

00:05:56.990 --> 00:06:00.450
Another thing is having a transparent road
map, so keeping your users in the loop.

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:03.010
And there's just all these
different kind of values.

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:06.010
There isn't one core value,
which is also why it's kind of hard to.

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:08.220
&gt;&gt; That sounds like a mistake, a mistake.

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:11.720
Almost everyone who says,
we have all these different values.

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:13.560
There are all these different
reasons people like it.

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:17.543
It's really nice, if you can figure out
how that all abstracts into one message.

00:06:17.543 --> 00:06:20.064
And then build all the companies sales and

00:06:20.064 --> 00:06:22.442
marketing PR around [CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; One message.

00:06:22.442 --> 00:06:23.888
&gt;&gt; This is what it's about,

00:06:23.888 --> 00:06:28.107
rather than saying, there's six
different reasons to use the product.

00:06:28.107 --> 00:06:30.575
I think one of the things that like, so

00:06:30.575 --> 00:06:34.524
when you say you have a problem
with people integrating you.

00:06:34.524 --> 00:06:37.891
It's not that hard, it's just not
high enough on their priority list.

00:06:37.891 --> 00:06:39.380
And there's a few ways to solve this.

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:43.080
One way that works is to send
people around and say like hey,

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:45.370
we're going to send an engineer
to come help integrate with you.

00:06:45.370 --> 00:06:47.100
And it's not that the company can't do it.

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:51.450
It's just that you'd otherwise you'd
always be at the bottom of their backlog.

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:52.440
And that's one way.

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:57.130
The other is if it's like their
CEO tells them to go do it.

00:06:57.130 --> 00:06:59.830
Or they themselves decide,
I gotta go do this.

00:06:59.830 --> 00:07:01.130
It's really important.

00:07:01.130 --> 00:07:04.050
And that almost never comes
from a bunch of disparate

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:06.520
messages that are a bunch
of nice-to-haves.

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:12.500
It's one thing which is,
maybe in this case, I mean,

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:14.540
it's some version of user feedback.

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:16.410
And how if you don't have this,

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:19.640
you will be iterating more
slowly than your competitors.

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:22.920
&gt;&gt; Or that your users will feel
like you're more engaged with them,

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:24.570
listening to the features
that they want built.

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:27.550
&gt;&gt; I have two big questions.

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:32.610
One, I think back of the envelope
math is always really important.

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:35.600
And I think,
I worry that this is the kind of

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:37.710
business where if you're
mostly selling to start ups.

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:40.899
And you're selling it a hundred bucks,
is it a month or a year?

00:07:40.899 --> 00:07:41.537
&gt;&gt; A month.

00:07:41.537 --> 00:07:45.728
&gt;&gt; A month, like how big can this
business actually get off of this model?

00:07:45.728 --> 00:07:49.607
Have you thought about that?

00:07:49.607 --> 00:07:51.652
&gt;&gt; We have thought about it.

00:07:51.652 --> 00:07:55.580
I mean I think,
like just doing napkin math.

00:07:55.580 --> 00:07:59.280
I think with a startup you usually want
to arrive somewhere around 100 million

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:00.540
annual revenue.

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:04.902
And so for us Like right now it's
looking like we'd be going for

00:08:04.902 --> 00:08:07.541
10,000 people paying us $100 a month.

00:08:07.541 --> 00:08:10.180
&gt;&gt; And does that feel doable?

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:13.147
&gt;&gt; Maybe it was 100,000, yeah.

00:08:13.147 --> 00:08:14.633
&gt;&gt; Okay, there's a difference there.

00:08:14.633 --> 00:08:15.672
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:08:15.672 --> 00:08:16.657
&gt;&gt; I mean I think the number-

00:08:16.657 --> 00:08:17.990
&gt;&gt; I think it is 100,000 for a $100-

00:08:17.990 --> 00:08:19.302
&gt;&gt; Yeah, 100,000, sorry.

00:08:19.302 --> 00:08:23.726
Sorry, I think the number of
companies that need feedback in

00:08:23.726 --> 00:08:25.635
the number of websites and

00:08:25.635 --> 00:08:28.159
mobile apps is increasing and-
&gt;&gt; But

00:08:28.159 --> 00:08:30.368
do you feel there's a 100,000 steps?

00:08:30.368 --> 00:08:34.474
So, part of it, it's like, do you feel
like you're going to have to own like 50 %

00:08:34.474 --> 00:08:36.272
of the startup market to get there?

00:08:36.272 --> 00:08:40.936
Or do you think that there's enough demand
out there that you can actually get to

00:08:40.936 --> 00:08:43.871
the market without
dominating the entire thing?

00:08:43.871 --> 00:08:45.447
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:08:45.447 --> 00:08:47.851
&gt;&gt; Right, I think as an aside to this,

00:08:47.851 --> 00:08:52.997
we've been getting a lot of feedback
that we can actually charge a lot more.

00:08:52.997 --> 00:08:56.498
And so I think there's a few features that
we can build out that will help a lot and-

00:08:56.498 --> 00:08:57.648
&gt;&gt; Do you use your own product?

00:08:57.648 --> 00:09:00.223
Do you have a-
&gt;&gt; Yeah, [LAUGH] definitely.

00:09:00.223 --> 00:09:03.149
That's a good way to figure
out if it's providing value.

00:09:03.149 --> 00:09:05.271
&gt;&gt; Again, maybe the answer
is to charge a lot more, and

00:09:05.271 --> 00:09:07.842
maybe the answer is that there
are a 100,000 start ups.

00:09:07.842 --> 00:09:11.225
Maybe the answer is that you need to tweak
the product in a way that you can appeal

00:09:11.225 --> 00:09:12.926
to a lot of people outside of start ups.

00:09:12.926 --> 00:09:18.480
But I think start ups almost never
just do the swag on this early enough.

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:24.083
And it really informs product strategy
which also gets to my next big question.

00:09:24.083 --> 00:09:28.428
This feels like the kind of service
that is interchangeable, and

00:09:28.428 --> 00:09:31.667
that a lot of people can
build a similar service.

00:09:31.667 --> 00:09:34.993
And if one company uses product A and
another company uses product B,

00:09:34.993 --> 00:09:37.643
there's no reason for
a third company to prefer A or B.

00:09:37.643 --> 00:09:40.072
There's no network effect,
there's no monopoly.

00:09:40.072 --> 00:09:45.196
Now I can imagine ways that you could
build one of those into the product,

00:09:45.196 --> 00:09:49.664
where there are sort of cross
company incentives to use yours.

00:09:49.664 --> 00:09:52.114
But what is your plan to be a monopoly and

00:09:52.114 --> 00:09:56.665
not another SaaS business that just
gets competed down to zero margins?

00:09:56.665 --> 00:09:57.368
&gt;&gt; That's a good question.

00:09:57.368 --> 00:10:00.713
I think-
&gt;&gt; And if you don't have an answer yet,

00:10:00.713 --> 00:10:03.735
it's also fine [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; I mean I think, our strategy so

00:10:03.735 --> 00:10:06.336
far is to go after influencers, and

00:10:06.336 --> 00:10:11.820
these are including people who have
users that would also use our product.

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:15.804
So, for example,
we go after open source projects and

00:10:15.804 --> 00:10:18.585
tools like Optimizer, for example.

00:10:18.585 --> 00:10:19.883
&gt;&gt; Do you give it free
to open source projects?

00:10:19.883 --> 00:10:23.152
&gt;&gt; Open source projects are free, yeah.

00:10:23.152 --> 00:10:26.334
As an alternative to GitHub and
GitHub issues, and

00:10:26.334 --> 00:10:31.001
they're bringing in us a lot of people,
I think that's a good move for us.

00:10:31.001 --> 00:10:36.266
But so far, I think our strategy is really
to put our product in front of the public.

00:10:36.266 --> 00:10:40.882
It's powered by Canny and
that's kind of where we're at with that.

00:10:40.882 --> 00:10:44.347
&gt;&gt; So,
do you have any thoughts about this?

00:10:44.347 --> 00:10:45.000
&gt;&gt; One little thought.

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:47.361
I think one thing that we've found so
far is that,

00:10:47.361 --> 00:10:49.850
there are other products
on the market that do this.

00:10:49.850 --> 00:10:51.281
And during our Product Hunt launch,

00:10:51.281 --> 00:10:53.670
we got people coming from those
products wanting to use us.

00:10:53.670 --> 00:10:57.690
And the thing that they mentioned liking
about us more is design and simplicity.

00:10:57.690 --> 00:11:00.721
And I honestly feel like our
product out of all the products in

00:11:00.721 --> 00:11:03.702
the market right now is the best
designed and the simplest.

00:11:03.702 --> 00:11:05.119
[CROSSTALK] For exactly what we do.

00:11:05.119 --> 00:11:07.159
&gt;&gt; I believe you that that's true,
but here is the problem.

00:11:07.159 --> 00:11:10.922
I'd say like one out of three start ups
I meet with says, we're like this other

00:11:10.922 --> 00:11:16.130
thing, except we're better designed, we're
more beautiful, and we're simpler to use.

00:11:16.130 --> 00:11:18.730
And it may be true that you are,
I believe that it is.

00:11:18.730 --> 00:11:21.120
But someone else can come
do that same thing to you.

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:23.614
&gt;&gt; More importantly, or
not necessarily more importantly, but

00:11:23.614 --> 00:11:26.857
oftentimes you start off very simple and
then you start having to build features,

00:11:26.857 --> 00:11:28.278
because you see that they're missing.

00:11:28.278 --> 00:11:31.717
And then somebody else comes in and
says, we're now the simplest.

00:11:31.717 --> 00:11:35.968
&gt;&gt; But in any case, it's really good.

00:11:35.968 --> 00:11:39.570
You've built a product that people love,
you've got them to pay for it.

00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:42.470
And you have a growth channel
that you believe will work.

00:11:42.470 --> 00:11:46.210
That puts you in the top of few percent
of all startups, so that's great.

00:11:46.210 --> 00:11:50.261
But this is the time where your biggest
problem now, part of that and the one

00:11:50.261 --> 00:11:54.633
thing you should be thinking about is what
can I do early to build a product that a,

00:11:54.633 --> 00:11:57.163
can eventually address
of very large market.

00:11:57.163 --> 00:12:01.285
And b,
how can I build some sort of monopoly?

00:12:01.285 --> 00:12:04.826
How can I build something where
because this company uses my product,

00:12:04.826 --> 00:12:07.960
this other company is going to be
more likely to use it, as well?

00:12:07.960 --> 00:12:12.539
And design in simplicity, although
everyone says it and it's kind of true,

00:12:12.539 --> 00:12:15.196
is usually not a long
term win at some point.

00:12:15.196 --> 00:12:17.618
And it may just be that once
you're integrated into a company,

00:12:17.618 --> 00:12:20.652
you're impossible to rip out, and then
you're going to win the mind share war.

00:12:20.652 --> 00:12:21.865
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; That could be it.

00:12:21.865 --> 00:12:22.612
&gt;&gt; That's what we're seeing, actually.

00:12:22.612 --> 00:12:23.498
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:12:23.498 --> 00:12:25.686
&gt;&gt; A better answer, though,

00:12:25.686 --> 00:12:30.729
would be if there's some way that
you can use aggregated data,

00:12:30.729 --> 00:12:35.789
or learnings, across all companies,
or aggregate or share.

00:12:35.789 --> 00:12:38.821
The kind of users that leave feedback
on products tend to do it across all

00:12:38.821 --> 00:12:40.398
the products they build, they use.

00:12:40.398 --> 00:12:43.801
So there's probably like a very small
percentage of users that give feedback,

00:12:43.801 --> 00:12:46.020
and they do it across all the products.

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:50.790
So my sense is that somewhere in there,
there is the strategy to build a monopoly.

00:12:50.790 --> 00:12:54.450
&gt;&gt; But you want to figure
out what that core is and

00:12:55.530 --> 00:12:58.370
build that into the core of the product.

00:12:58.370 --> 00:13:01.392
So that you have a long-term,
the principle strategy.

00:13:01.392 --> 00:13:05.351
&gt;&gt; Yeah, this is actually very interesting
because we probably started at more of

00:13:05.351 --> 00:13:09.089
an aggregate level, where everyone
could post feedback about anything.

00:13:09.089 --> 00:13:12.950
And that was hard, because companies
weren't on board first and so.

00:13:12.950 --> 00:13:14.031
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that doesn't feel right either.

00:13:14.031 --> 00:13:16.877
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so right now we've decided
to go this other way for now.

00:13:16.877 --> 00:13:21.564
But there is a possibility that
there one day we're connecting

00:13:21.564 --> 00:13:26.260
all the users if they post on
multiple products that use Canny.

00:13:26.260 --> 00:13:27.669
We can connect all those people,

00:13:27.669 --> 00:13:30.247
so maybe they're some potential
there eventually, yeah.

00:13:30.247 --> 00:13:31.876
&gt;&gt; And why is it named Canny?

00:13:31.876 --> 00:13:34.268
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Canny was really hard to find.

00:13:34.268 --> 00:13:37.076
We went through many different names, but

00:13:37.076 --> 00:13:39.968
a core part of our
product is the subdomain.

00:13:39.968 --> 00:13:46.277
And we didn't want to use something that
was like get this or try this, use this.

00:13:46.277 --> 00:13:46.866
&gt;&gt; Short and sweet.

00:13:46.866 --> 00:13:48.644
&gt;&gt; Yeah so something short and sweet.

00:13:48.644 --> 00:13:52.785
We got canny.io so it's not the worst,
but it's not the best.

00:13:52.785 --> 00:13:53.990
&gt;&gt; You can still change,
it's not too late.

00:13:53.990 --> 00:13:55.945
&gt;&gt; We can still change [CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; I thought it was a play on uncanny

00:13:55.945 --> 00:13:57.899
&gt;&gt; Yeah, [LAUGH] actually they're kind of

00:13:57.899 --> 00:13:58.972
different but yeah.

00:13:58.972 --> 00:14:03.707
It means to make good judgment, and so
I think that kind of plays in as well,

00:14:03.707 --> 00:14:04.395
but yeah.

00:14:04.395 --> 00:14:06.604
&gt;&gt; Okay, if it's working, if people
remember it, if they spell it right,

00:14:06.604 --> 00:14:07.187
that's all good.

00:14:07.187 --> 00:14:09.888
Anything else you'd like to talk about?

00:14:09.888 --> 00:14:16.509
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean in general I think
to your point about broad use cases.

00:14:16.509 --> 00:14:20.128
After our Product Hunt launch it came
out with a lot of people using Canny for

00:14:20.128 --> 00:14:21.173
different reasons.

00:14:21.173 --> 00:14:23.643
And so I think-
&gt;&gt; Is there one reason that's surfacing?

00:14:23.643 --> 00:14:28.815
&gt;&gt; Like focusing on, yeah so
I mean the top one is feature requests,

00:14:28.815 --> 00:14:30.985
which is what we expected.

00:14:30.985 --> 00:14:33.079
But also I think pricing
is one of the big ones,

00:14:33.079 --> 00:14:34.975
like figuring out what our main value is.

00:14:34.975 --> 00:14:38.047
And that's what we're trying to do,
we're trying to isolate that one thing.

00:14:38.047 --> 00:14:40.917
To especially market it on our homepage,

00:14:40.917 --> 00:14:44.445
how do we pull people in
with that one value, yeah.

00:14:44.445 --> 00:14:47.346
&gt;&gt; Feature requests don't
sound quite as good as like,

00:14:49.573 --> 00:14:52.435
&gt;&gt; User I don't know.

00:14:52.435 --> 00:14:55.488
I would be willing to, this is the place
where I believe in A B testing.

00:14:55.488 --> 00:14:58.910
I don't believe in it for everything, but
in terms of messaging and communication,

00:14:58.910 --> 00:15:02.139
and what are those four words you put big
on your website when people come there for

00:15:02.139 --> 00:15:02.828
the first time.

00:15:02.828 --> 00:15:04.823
This is the place I
believe in A B testing.

00:15:04.823 --> 00:15:07.960
And if feature requests are really
what people want, then great.

00:15:07.960 --> 00:15:09.809
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK] put it right up front.

00:15:09.809 --> 00:15:11.850
&gt;&gt; If that's the thing that's
working you should at least,

00:15:11.850 --> 00:15:13.736
that's at least the candidate for
the right answer.

00:15:13.736 --> 00:15:15.250
All right,
we should move on to the next group.

00:15:15.250 --> 00:15:16.471
Thank you so much.
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:15:16.471 --> 00:15:25.171
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; How's it going.

00:15:25.171 --> 00:15:26.671
Chris.
&gt;&gt; Hey.

00:15:26.671 --> 00:15:27.651
&gt;&gt; Chris.
&gt;&gt; How's it going?

00:15:30.902 --> 00:15:33.038
By the way, I don't know what any of
these companies do ahead of time.

00:15:33.038 --> 00:15:35.171
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Get a live reaction from me, so.

00:15:35.171 --> 00:15:36.469
Do you want to introduce yourself?

00:15:36.469 --> 00:15:38.045
&gt;&gt; Hi everybody, my name is Chris Beltran.

00:15:38.045 --> 00:15:40.963
I'm the CEO and Founder of.

00:15:40.963 --> 00:15:45.295
What we do is connect home owners,
private property owners, and

00:15:45.295 --> 00:15:48.149
all sorts of parking
lot owners to our app.

00:15:48.149 --> 00:15:51.917
And which our app does is it aggregates,
essentially, their parking spaces.

00:15:51.917 --> 00:15:53.850
Let's them lease it out.

00:15:53.850 --> 00:15:58.110
The owners are able to make some passive
income and the parkers are able to

00:15:58.110 --> 00:16:01.190
just go to that parking spot whenever
they want to, easily and quickly.

00:16:01.190 --> 00:16:05.320
So this tool was meant for
specifically San Francisco,

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:09.600
highly industrialized cities in
which people need to park easier and

00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:12.680
faster rather than circling around-
&gt;&gt; It's like Airbnb for parking.

00:16:12.680 --> 00:16:13.610
&gt;&gt; Essentially, yes it is.

00:16:13.610 --> 00:16:15.350
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I've wanted to see
something like this happen for

00:16:15.350 --> 00:16:18.550
a long time, because it feels like
in urban cities, like San Francisco,

00:16:18.550 --> 00:16:20.450
there's just not enough street parking.

00:16:20.450 --> 00:16:24.790
&gt;&gt; That is, but right now more

00:16:24.790 --> 00:16:29.410
of the issue is this is a perceptive
problem or is this an actual problem?

00:16:29.410 --> 00:16:31.450
&gt;&gt; So are you live in the city right now?

00:16:31.450 --> 00:16:32.260
We are not live in the city.

00:16:32.260 --> 00:16:34.215
We are currently building
out the MVP right now.

00:16:34.215 --> 00:16:35.073
&gt;&gt; Got it.

00:16:35.073 --> 00:16:36.230
&gt;&gt; Unfortunately my
co-founder is not here.

00:16:36.230 --> 00:16:38.336
She's actually taking
a vacation through Mexico.

00:16:38.336 --> 00:16:39.733
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I think she's doing some traveling.

00:16:39.733 --> 00:16:43.571
So, mind you, I'm not a solo founder,
I do have a co-founder.

00:16:43.571 --> 00:16:47.076
&gt;&gt; That's all right.

00:16:47.076 --> 00:16:49.730
Have you been talking to
homeowners in the meantime?

00:16:49.730 --> 00:16:52.200
Are they willing to rent
out their parking spots?

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:55.305
&gt;&gt; You know what, the funny thing is I'm
actually hitting the ground running.

00:16:55.305 --> 00:16:59.617
And I'm physically going out to these
homes and or private parking lots, and

00:16:59.617 --> 00:17:03.410
pitching to them my idea and
say hey why don't you use our platform?

00:17:03.410 --> 00:17:06.050
This is what it's going to look like and
I'm representing our mock ups

00:17:06.050 --> 00:17:10.050
to essentially say this is the platform
that you would essentially be using.

00:17:10.050 --> 00:17:11.560
&gt;&gt; And what do they tell you?

00:17:11.560 --> 00:17:13.520
&gt;&gt; More often than not,
they say this is awesome,

00:17:13.520 --> 00:17:15.290
why haven't anybody else created this?

00:17:15.290 --> 00:17:18.180
&gt;&gt; Are they willing to say hey,
I'm ready to pay?

00:17:18.180 --> 00:17:19.530
Or would they pay you?

00:17:19.530 --> 00:17:22.560
No, you would pay them, you would
do a rough share with them, right?

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:23.060
&gt;&gt; Correct.
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:17:23.060 --> 00:17:25.015
&gt;&gt; So essentially a percentage of

00:17:25.015 --> 00:17:29.151
sales that occur when the commuter
parks in their parking space,

00:17:29.151 --> 00:17:33.409
we'll collect 15%, they collect 85%.
&gt;&gt; So can you sign them up.

00:17:33.409 --> 00:17:34.359
I mean part of the thing for

00:17:34.359 --> 00:17:35.519
you is that when you launch in a city,

00:17:35.519 --> 00:17:38.360
you're going to want as much critical
mass as you want for parking spots.

00:17:38.360 --> 00:17:40.040
So are they willing to sign up and
say hey,

00:17:40.040 --> 00:17:42.390
my parking spot is yours
the moment you're ready?

00:17:42.390 --> 00:17:44.900
&gt;&gt; Currently what we're doing
is we're creating lists and

00:17:44.900 --> 00:17:46.210
we're pitching the idea to them.

00:17:46.210 --> 00:17:50.306
So essentially what we're trying to do
is we're trying to curate all these

00:17:50.306 --> 00:17:54.729
homeowners and private property owners
throughout San Francisco that play with

00:17:54.729 --> 00:17:57.656
San Francisco standards,
because you cannot park or

00:17:57.656 --> 00:17:59.700
impede over onto the sidewalk.

00:17:59.700 --> 00:18:02.350
So obviously,
there's some things that we have to do

00:18:02.350 --> 00:18:05.050
to double-check to make sure
that they're completely legal.

00:18:05.050 --> 00:18:05.820
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:18:05.820 --> 00:18:08.930
&gt;&gt; But otherwise, what we're doing
is we're collecting data from them.

00:18:08.930 --> 00:18:11.340
And yes, they are more than
willing to sign up with us.

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:13.320
&gt;&gt; When do you think you'll launch?

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:16.410
&gt;&gt; I pretty much project it
to launch within six months,

00:18:16.410 --> 00:18:17.960
and-
&gt;&gt; Why so long?

00:18:17.960 --> 00:18:19.530
&gt;&gt; Why so long?

00:18:19.530 --> 00:18:20.720
&gt;&gt; How about six days?

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:24.710
&gt;&gt; I would love to build this out in six
days, but currently I'm not a technical

00:18:24.710 --> 00:18:27.482
founder, so that was, or a technical-
&gt;&gt; Is your co-founder?

00:18:27.482 --> 00:18:31.710
&gt;&gt; My co-founder is technical,
so she has the tools and

00:18:31.710 --> 00:18:34.370
resources and
clout to build this essentially.

00:18:34.370 --> 00:18:37.020
But she's on vacation,
I wish she was here.

00:18:37.020 --> 00:18:38.450
&gt;&gt; When is she back?

00:18:38.450 --> 00:18:40.710
She should be back within the week.

00:18:40.710 --> 00:18:41.840
&gt;&gt; A full six days after that.

00:18:41.840 --> 00:18:43.830
&gt;&gt; I know, I would love to push this.

00:18:43.830 --> 00:18:46.585
&gt;&gt; One of the things
that's really hard is,

00:18:49.839 --> 00:18:52.899
Like your putting this product of yourself
out there when you launch a startup and

00:18:52.899 --> 00:18:55.160
no one wants to do it until they
feel like it's really good.

00:18:56.420 --> 00:18:58.940
But in six months,
the world will look entirely different.

00:18:58.940 --> 00:19:00.892
And every good idea you have,
someone else has.

00:19:00.892 --> 00:19:03.133
And now a lot of people are going to hear
about this because we're talking about it.

00:19:03.133 --> 00:19:05.191
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Don't steal my idea.

00:19:05.191 --> 00:19:07.710
All of you, don't steal it.

00:19:07.710 --> 00:19:11.943
&gt;&gt; I mean stealing the idea is not,
so it's like-

00:19:11.943 --> 00:19:13.430
&gt;&gt; MBA's for all of you.

00:19:13.430 --> 00:19:15.580
&gt;&gt; Speed and execution really matter and

00:19:15.580 --> 00:19:18.560
actually you could 10
million people the idea.

00:19:18.560 --> 00:19:20.790
And probably all of them would
be busy with their own things.

00:19:20.790 --> 00:19:24.390
But a few of them will be
interested at least and

00:19:24.390 --> 00:19:27.930
there's this issue of speed and
how quickly you can build and iterate.

00:19:27.930 --> 00:19:31.730
And especially in the marketplace,
there is a big first mover advantage.

00:19:31.730 --> 00:19:36.460
So even if it's not the version you want,
my most important piece of advice to you

00:19:36.460 --> 00:19:41.960
would be to get this launched really
quickly in an ugly first version.

00:19:41.960 --> 00:19:43.580
And then you'll learn so

00:19:43.580 --> 00:19:46.430
much about how to build a better
version that you can go from there.

00:19:46.430 --> 00:19:47.782
It's great that you're
talking to customers,

00:19:47.782 --> 00:19:50.010
that is clearly the number
one thing to go do.

00:19:50.010 --> 00:19:52.380
And getting people signed up so
that you have inventory.

00:19:52.380 --> 00:19:56.298
But I would think about how
you bring the launch in,

00:19:56.298 --> 00:20:01.508
even if it's a soft launch that
you're embarrassed about by a lot.

00:20:01.508 --> 00:20:03.615
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
especially if the problem is acute enough,

00:20:03.615 --> 00:20:06.970
if it's something that people really
struggle with, finding parking spots.

00:20:06.970 --> 00:20:08.940
They won't care how pretty the app is or

00:20:08.940 --> 00:20:11.560
how ugly the app is as long as
they can find the parking spots.

00:20:11.560 --> 00:20:17.327
So having something live, even if it's
just a website wrapped in a little icon.

00:20:17.327 --> 00:20:19.740
&gt;&gt; That's literally what we're doing is
we're targeting certain neighborhoods.

00:20:19.740 --> 00:20:22.270
So for instance,
there's neighborhoods that,

00:20:22.270 --> 00:20:23.810
I'm sure you've been to
San Francisco multiple times.

00:20:23.810 --> 00:20:24.460
&gt;&gt; I live there.

00:20:24.460 --> 00:20:25.390
&gt;&gt; You live there, obviously.

00:20:25.390 --> 00:20:30.490
Haight District is one of them which is
severely impacted nine times out of ten,

00:20:30.490 --> 00:20:31.350
even during the week.

00:20:31.350 --> 00:20:32.510
&gt;&gt; How do you know?

00:20:32.510 --> 00:20:34.850
&gt;&gt; I've been there physically and
had a check board out.

00:20:34.850 --> 00:20:36.940
And checking the times and
stand there for eight hours a day.

00:20:38.410 --> 00:20:42.510
But there are times where it does free
up a little bit, but for the most part.

00:20:42.510 --> 00:20:45.300
&gt;&gt; So I understand how you're going
to get the parking places on board

00:20:45.300 --> 00:20:47.120
which is I think the harder
half of the marketplace.

00:20:47.120 --> 00:20:49.253
But you still need people
that want to park,

00:20:49.253 --> 00:20:51.470
how are you going to get those people?

00:20:51.470 --> 00:20:52.190
&gt;&gt; So what we're going to do

00:20:52.190 --> 00:20:53.949
is we're going to reach out
to people during festivals.

00:20:55.060 --> 00:20:56.960
There's tons of festivals,
there's outside lands.

00:20:56.960 --> 00:21:00.603
There's some that go on on Treasure Island
even though as everyone knows that's out

00:21:00.603 --> 00:21:01.660
of our reach.

00:21:01.660 --> 00:21:03.770
But there's many that happen
around Golden Gate park.

00:21:03.770 --> 00:21:05.186
There's Bay to Breakers,

00:21:05.186 --> 00:21:08.641
there's tons of festivals we're
going to be pitching this around.

00:21:08.641 --> 00:21:12.810
Or some type of theme, or some type
of festival, some type of ball game.

00:21:12.810 --> 00:21:15.890
&gt;&gt; I worry that those A,
don't happen frequently enough.

00:21:15.890 --> 00:21:17.860
And B,
those are people who are not looking for

00:21:17.860 --> 00:21:20.320
parking super-regularly in the city.

00:21:20.320 --> 00:21:23.550
It would be interesting if you could
figure out a way to find people for

00:21:23.550 --> 00:21:26.830
who this is truly a hair on fire problem.

00:21:26.830 --> 00:21:29.644
That drive to the city every day and
have to park, or live in the city and

00:21:29.644 --> 00:21:30.850
don't have a parking place.

00:21:30.850 --> 00:21:35.120
That does not strike me as
the most urgent need, or

00:21:35.120 --> 00:21:41.570
the best way to find people who are really
feeling this problem every day.

00:21:41.570 --> 00:21:45.020
Which there are a lot of,
this is a great problem to be solving.

00:21:45.020 --> 00:21:47.500
I would think about how you find,

00:21:47.500 --> 00:21:50.440
I would think about other
strategies to find a lot of people.

00:21:50.440 --> 00:21:54.770
The festival thing, I just worry
there aren't enough festivals and

00:21:54.770 --> 00:21:58.080
there aren't enough people at those that
have this as a regular ongoing problem.

00:21:58.080 --> 00:22:01.460
You want the people who
are going to be daily, or

00:22:01.460 --> 00:22:05.200
at least weekly users of this, and
I bet you can come up with better ways.

00:22:05.200 --> 00:22:07.790
Well I think if you have feet on the
ground and actually surveying the place,

00:22:07.790 --> 00:22:09.710
I would actually ask the people
who are parking, why are you here?

00:22:09.710 --> 00:22:10.850
&gt;&gt; That's exactly what I'm doing.

00:22:10.850 --> 00:22:12.740
&gt;&gt; And see, so what's the feedback?

00:22:12.740 --> 00:22:14.580
Are they working there?

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:17.015
&gt;&gt; So nine times out of ten,
people are either coming into the city, so

00:22:17.015 --> 00:22:19.250
there are people that are not
living in the city, obviously.

00:22:19.250 --> 00:22:22.866
because a lot of people who live in
the city don't necessarily own a car,

00:22:22.866 --> 00:22:23.569
so-
&gt;&gt; But they're commuting for

00:22:23.569 --> 00:22:24.170
what, are they commuting for work?

00:22:24.170 --> 00:22:25.170
&gt;&gt; They're commuting for work and

00:22:25.170 --> 00:22:27.500
sometimes they're just commuting
to do some contract work.

00:22:27.500 --> 00:22:29.050
They've gotta come into the city every so
often,

00:22:29.050 --> 00:22:30.540
there's people that come in for meetings.

00:22:31.600 --> 00:22:33.410
A whole host of reasons that they come in.

00:22:33.410 --> 00:22:35.502
&gt;&gt; I mean these people are driving
today and parking today,

00:22:35.502 --> 00:22:37.080
maybe they'll do it more if it's easier.

00:22:37.080 --> 00:22:39.770
But can you put signs in the parking lot,
in the parking spaces,

00:22:39.770 --> 00:22:42.050
that say to park here, download this?

00:22:42.050 --> 00:22:43.640
&gt;&gt; Yeah, essentially, just- [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That'll work.

00:22:43.640 --> 00:22:46.540
&gt;&gt; So when that time comes, and
which we do have a product launch.

00:22:46.540 --> 00:22:49.140
I'm going to create maybe
little signs at first,

00:22:49.140 --> 00:22:51.740
something cheap just to get started.

00:22:51.740 --> 00:22:54.882
Maybe laminated or whatnot,
to put it on those places where it needs

00:22:54.882 --> 00:22:56.659
to be [CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; You need something,

00:22:56.659 --> 00:22:58.890
in a lot of cases it is an impulse buy,
right.

00:22:58.890 --> 00:23:01.910
They are going to be looking for parking,
they didn't find the street parking and

00:23:01.910 --> 00:23:04.750
now they're ready to buy whatever
it is you're giving them.

00:23:04.750 --> 00:23:07.390
&gt;&gt; I have a general thought for
you about this kind of marketplace.

00:23:09.340 --> 00:23:13.180
Airbnb is a really good business
because if you own a space and

00:23:13.180 --> 00:23:15.900
you're renting it out you can only
put it on basically one platform,

00:23:15.900 --> 00:23:17.820
if you're going to use
the instant book feature.

00:23:17.820 --> 00:23:22.840
And if you're a guest, you really care
if you get this particular house or

00:23:22.840 --> 00:23:23.670
this particular house.

00:23:23.670 --> 00:23:24.730
They're not interchangeable.

00:23:25.850 --> 00:23:28.730
Ride sharing, if you're a driver
you can drive from any service.

00:23:28.730 --> 00:23:29.650
You turn the apps on and off.

00:23:29.650 --> 00:23:33.540
But if you're a passenger you don't
care much which car you get in.

00:23:33.540 --> 00:23:38.180
And it's really nice if you can somehow
have the former kind of marketplace,

00:23:38.180 --> 00:23:40.830
where there's not just
perfect substitution and

00:23:40.830 --> 00:23:44.290
everyone is switching between
each service depending on price.

00:23:44.290 --> 00:23:48.680
And so if you can do something with, I
think this is like actually a very big and

00:23:48.680 --> 00:23:51.680
important insight about
marketplaces that most people miss.

00:23:51.680 --> 00:23:55.010
And if you can do something where people
can only list their space on your

00:23:55.010 --> 00:23:58.080
marketplace because you're going to
have around the clock Instant booking or

00:23:58.080 --> 00:24:02.140
something, that would be really
important to figure out early.

00:24:02.140 --> 00:24:06.190
And a very valuable tweak that
doesn't sound that big but

00:24:06.190 --> 00:24:10.360
it's actually a huge difference to
the long-term liability of the business.

00:24:10.360 --> 00:24:14.699
&gt;&gt; I think that's a good take on it.

00:24:14.699 --> 00:24:18.412
So right now as far as the 24-hour booking
and what not, I want to give the property

00:24:18.412 --> 00:24:21.050
owners or homeowners the ability
to set their own times.

00:24:21.050 --> 00:24:23.380
&gt;&gt; Sure.
&gt;&gt; So sometimes maybe some owners, or

00:24:23.380 --> 00:24:26.960
property owners,
say we'll just say Wells Fargo.

00:24:26.960 --> 00:24:29.985
They probably set theirs a little bit
later after business hours are closed.

00:24:29.985 --> 00:24:31.941
&gt;&gt; But do you think that you'll be able
to get businesses to give up their

00:24:31.941 --> 00:24:32.626
parking spots as well?

00:24:32.626 --> 00:24:35.160
&gt;&gt; I would love to reach out to businesses
to give out their parking spots as well.

00:24:35.160 --> 00:24:39.015
&gt;&gt; Interesting, because I was thinking
that this is more around homeowners or

00:24:39.015 --> 00:24:41.337
people who have extra spaces on their own.

00:24:41.337 --> 00:24:42.900
But businesses could
be really interesting.

00:24:42.900 --> 00:24:45.690
It's a very different sale now
because now you really have to go and

00:24:45.690 --> 00:24:49.050
convince whoever the property manager
is or somebody else to actually do it.

00:24:49.050 --> 00:24:51.080
&gt;&gt; Correct, correct.

00:24:51.080 --> 00:24:51.930
&gt;&gt; Go ahead.

00:24:51.930 --> 00:24:56.060
&gt;&gt; Sorry, just one thought here, which is
I would pick one of them in the beginning,

00:24:56.060 --> 00:24:57.970
because you won't be able to sell to both.

00:24:57.970 --> 00:25:01.354
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I especially wanted
focus on possibly residential and

00:25:01.354 --> 00:25:04.437
private lots first, and
then start slowly building out.

00:25:04.437 --> 00:25:06.128
&gt;&gt; Do you have any sense yet for pricing?

00:25:06.128 --> 00:25:06.741
&gt;&gt; Yes, so

00:25:06.741 --> 00:25:12.265
[LAUGH] I pretty much wanted to model
this after the meters in San Francisco.

00:25:12.265 --> 00:25:14.625
And right now the meters are all timed.

00:25:14.625 --> 00:25:18.790
SFpark actually did a survey in
which they increased meter pricing.

00:25:18.790 --> 00:25:21.121
So similar to variable-based pricing, so

00:25:21.121 --> 00:25:23.582
that's what we're going to
pretty much mimic.

00:25:23.582 --> 00:25:28.080
Essentially we want to be competing with
the meters themselves for residential.

00:25:28.080 --> 00:25:31.251
For private lots obviously we're going to
be competing with private lot prices.

00:25:31.251 --> 00:25:35.232
So we're just going to base our model
off of the model that San Francisco has

00:25:35.232 --> 00:25:36.830
already done.

00:25:36.830 --> 00:25:38.430
&gt;&gt; And people would be able to park,

00:25:38.430 --> 00:25:42.647
I assume, as long as they want and
the property owner is [INAUDIBLE].

00:25:42.647 --> 00:25:44.660
&gt;&gt; As long as the property owners set
their times to say, eight to five,

00:25:44.660 --> 00:25:47.610
they can park eight to five and
it'll be on an hourly rate.

00:25:47.610 --> 00:25:48.980
&gt;&gt; What's the legal situation of this?

00:25:48.980 --> 00:25:51.110
I assume it's okay if
it's private property.

00:25:51.110 --> 00:25:52.780
&gt;&gt; It is legal, it is completely legal.

00:25:52.780 --> 00:25:55.770
&gt;&gt; Okay.
&gt;&gt; I looked into it, I talked to an SF law

00:25:55.770 --> 00:26:01.530
attorney, specifically for San Francisco
themselves, and I've asked them, can I?

00:26:01.530 --> 00:26:02.511
It's kind of a funny question.

00:26:02.511 --> 00:26:03.200
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:26:03.200 --> 00:26:07.584
&gt;&gt; The story goes is I called them up,
I said, hey my my dad is bedridden but

00:26:07.584 --> 00:26:12.115
he's not able to work, he doesn't
get enough from social security, and

00:26:12.115 --> 00:26:15.286
essentially he needs to
make some passive income.

00:26:15.286 --> 00:26:17.370
Is it okay if we could rent
this space out to our neighbor?

00:26:17.370 --> 00:26:21.980
And they said, as long as it's his
house we don't see any issue with it.

00:26:21.980 --> 00:26:22.944
&gt;&gt; Chris.
&gt;&gt; That's great.

00:26:24.110 --> 00:26:28.080
&gt;&gt; Again this is a really good idea,
if you don't move fast, it won't work.

00:26:28.080 --> 00:26:32.040
So I think it's always important as
a startup to know what the number one

00:26:32.040 --> 00:26:36.078
biggest problem in front of you is,
for you it's not shipping quickly.

00:26:36.078 --> 00:26:39.409
And so just get this working in
one neighborhood as quickly as you

00:26:39.409 --> 00:26:40.236
possibly can.

00:26:40.236 --> 00:26:43.980
&gt;&gt; Even if it's hacked up and
doesn't look that great.

00:26:43.980 --> 00:26:45.363
&gt;&gt; Okay.
&gt;&gt; Any other questions you

00:26:45.363 --> 00:26:46.113
want to discuss?

00:26:46.113 --> 00:26:47.560
&gt;&gt; I think that's it.

00:26:47.560 --> 00:26:48.720
&gt;&gt; Great.
&gt;&gt; I'm pretty good.

00:26:48.720 --> 00:26:49.425
&gt;&gt; I'm excited to try it out.

00:26:49.425 --> 00:26:50.730
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you.

00:26:50.730 --> 00:26:52.142
&gt;&gt; Thank you.
Email us when you want.

00:26:52.142 --> 00:26:56.009
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:27:02.820 --> 00:27:03.890
How's it going?

00:27:03.890 --> 00:27:04.920
I'm Yuri.
&gt;&gt; Phillip.

00:27:04.920 --> 00:27:05.600
&gt;&gt; Nice to meet you.
&gt;&gt; I'm Sam.

00:27:05.600 --> 00:27:06.530
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm Emma.
&gt;&gt; Yuri, nice to meet you.

00:27:06.530 --> 00:27:07.300
&gt;&gt; Hi.
&gt;&gt; Nice to meet you.

00:27:07.300 --> 00:27:10.797
&gt;&gt; What do you guys do?

00:27:10.797 --> 00:27:11.840
&gt;&gt; So, I'm Phillip.

00:27:13.430 --> 00:27:15.320
&gt;&gt; I'm Emma and we're MoonlightWork.

00:27:15.320 --> 00:27:18.770
So we're building a marketplace for
software engineers and

00:27:18.770 --> 00:27:23.350
designers to match up with companies
who need them ten hours at a time.

00:27:23.350 --> 00:27:25.270
&gt;&gt; So specifically ten hours at the time?

00:27:25.270 --> 00:27:27.551
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think we'll start
with ten hours at a time but

00:27:27.551 --> 00:27:30.403
some companies want more than that and
some people want more work.

00:27:30.403 --> 00:27:34.030
So if they want more time,
they can have more time.

00:27:34.030 --> 00:27:35.870
&gt;&gt; Is this launched, up and running?

00:27:35.870 --> 00:27:39.930
&gt;&gt; We have a prototype up and running,
we haven't had anybody else use it.

00:27:39.930 --> 00:27:42.309
But both of us have both-
&gt;&gt; Built projects for other people?

00:27:42.309 --> 00:27:44.050
That's a great way to start, that's good.

00:27:44.050 --> 00:27:46.580
&gt;&gt; Yes, so we started it because
we have this need ourselves.

00:27:46.580 --> 00:27:47.640
&gt;&gt; How do companies find you?

00:27:49.340 --> 00:27:52.421
&gt;&gt; There's been a variety of them,
some of them have been like friends

00:27:52.421 --> 00:27:55.125
of friends that heard that we
were doing contract work, and

00:27:55.125 --> 00:27:57.200
would come in and
assign jobs through there.

00:27:57.200 --> 00:27:59.807
Another thing is when we
shut down our last company,

00:27:59.807 --> 00:28:02.890
we open-sourced all the code and
so we had people coming in and

00:28:02.890 --> 00:28:05.900
paying us to deploy that code for
them on their own servers.

00:28:05.900 --> 00:28:07.390
They found us through GitHub basically.

00:28:07.390 --> 00:28:09.360
&gt;&gt; What kind of projects are people
having you build so far?

00:28:11.560 --> 00:28:14.400
&gt;&gt; So far it's a lot of people
who have a very specific need.

00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:17.180
So they're like I need
an expert in this language, or

00:28:17.180 --> 00:28:19.310
an expert that knows how to
build this thing really well.

00:28:19.310 --> 00:28:22.180
And so they're looking for someone who
there's not going to be much on boarding.

00:28:22.180 --> 00:28:24.532
They'll just going to be able
to jump in and do a project.

00:28:24.532 --> 00:28:28.141
&gt;&gt; Why did they choose you instead of
something like Upwork or Top Pal or

00:28:28.141 --> 00:28:30.500
one of the other kind of
outsourcing houses?

00:28:30.500 --> 00:28:33.980
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think the biggest
reason is because we're

00:28:33.980 --> 00:28:36.740
promoting it as something
that experts are working on.

00:28:36.740 --> 00:28:41.040
So these aren't just anyone in the world
who wants to make $15 an hour.

00:28:41.040 --> 00:28:44.534
This is someone who maybe works at
a Google or an Uber and they want to make

00:28:44.534 --> 00:28:48.340
a bunch of extra money on the side, but
they're really good at what they do.

00:28:48.340 --> 00:28:51.150
&gt;&gt; So you're going to have to
actually curate these people then.

00:28:51.150 --> 00:28:52.934
Is that something you're already doing?

00:28:52.934 --> 00:28:55.084
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
that's what we're starting to do and

00:28:55.084 --> 00:28:57.573
I think that's going to be
the biggest barrier for us,

00:28:57.573 --> 00:29:01.770
is being able to make sure that these
people are really quality workers.

00:29:01.770 --> 00:29:04.300
&gt;&gt; How much are you charging for
a ten hour project?

00:29:04.300 --> 00:29:08.360
&gt;&gt; So the contractors set their own rates
and then we're handling the invoicing and

00:29:08.360 --> 00:29:10.550
charging 15% on top of that.

00:29:10.550 --> 00:29:12.560
&gt;&gt; So but true marketplace.

00:29:12.560 --> 00:29:13.200
&gt;&gt; Correct, yeah.

00:29:13.200 --> 00:29:13.700
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:29:15.180 --> 00:29:19.855
Other than quality, given that
this is clearly a good business so

00:29:19.855 --> 00:29:24.190
a lot of people want in it and
a lot of people are already in it.

00:29:24.190 --> 00:29:27.949
How will you become the marketplace
where everyone goes for

00:29:27.949 --> 00:29:29.986
contracted engineering help?

00:29:29.986 --> 00:29:32.010
&gt;&gt; I think we can speak from our
own experience and say, like,

00:29:32.010 --> 00:29:34.441
there have been times in the past
when we have, like, student loans and

00:29:34.441 --> 00:29:36.880
would have worked extra time
every week to pay them off.

00:29:36.880 --> 00:29:40.935
And there's not really marketplaces out
there where people who want over 50 or

00:29:40.935 --> 00:29:45.440
$70 per hour can find short-term
projects that they can do on the side.

00:29:45.440 --> 00:29:49.184
&gt;&gt; So that's true on the developer side,
but how do you get the companies to come

00:29:49.184 --> 00:29:51.957
to you and say, when we need an expert,
we go to you guys?

00:29:51.957 --> 00:29:54.540
&gt;&gt; We're working on a couple
different ways of matching.

00:29:54.540 --> 00:29:57.416
But I think the short of it is that there
are very specialized needs that some

00:29:57.416 --> 00:29:58.110
companies have.

00:29:58.110 --> 00:30:00.900
And it doesn't even have
to be core to the product.

00:30:00.900 --> 00:30:04.750
Sometimes it's like, I need to set up
a development environment, or I want

00:30:04.750 --> 00:30:08.250
a landing page and our design team is
focused on an app, or something like that.

00:30:08.250 --> 00:30:12.030
&gt;&gt; I wonder if you want to become
known as the place to go to for

00:30:12.030 --> 00:30:14.110
a particular set of skills.

00:30:14.110 --> 00:30:17.781
Not for every expertise, but in the
beginning just a particular set where they

00:30:17.781 --> 00:30:20.850
just go to you and then you open
up additional verticals as you go.

00:30:20.850 --> 00:30:23.760
&gt;&gt; And for that,
we're looking at open-sourced projects.

00:30:23.760 --> 00:30:26.654
So an open source project is something
where it takes a lot of knowledge,

00:30:26.654 --> 00:30:29.131
a lot of experience to kind of
understand the ins and the outs.

00:30:29.131 --> 00:30:31.793
And so we tested that with our
own open source projects and

00:30:31.793 --> 00:30:34.950
got projects coming through there and
people signing up.

00:30:34.950 --> 00:30:37.811
And I think that matching people based
on very specific, open source projects.

00:30:37.811 --> 00:30:43.397
Not saying a Ruby programming language but
something like the Mongo DB bindings for

00:30:43.397 --> 00:30:46.610
Chef, for
something very specific like that.

00:30:48.940 --> 00:30:53.297
&gt;&gt; I think the insight of going
after high-end work and also for

00:30:53.297 --> 00:30:57.838
people who are just looking for
smaller pieces is really good.

00:30:57.838 --> 00:31:03.159
I wonder if you could pick projects
that particularly need that.

00:31:03.159 --> 00:31:07.641
Like projects that are harder to build and
take less time, sort of need specialized

00:31:07.641 --> 00:31:11.945
skills and are not the normal, like,
I just need people to grind through this.

00:31:11.945 --> 00:31:16.520
That would strike me as
a good initial focus.

00:31:16.520 --> 00:31:17.310
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:31:17.310 --> 00:31:19.860
&gt;&gt; What's the biggest problem in
front of the company right now?

00:31:19.860 --> 00:31:22.030
&gt;&gt; I think it's making sure that matching,

00:31:22.030 --> 00:31:24.990
I mean obviously every
marketplace has that issue.

00:31:24.990 --> 00:31:29.186
But making sure that the people we have
are worth what they're trying to get

00:31:29.186 --> 00:31:29.991
charged for.

00:31:29.991 --> 00:31:33.904
because we want to set a limit on how
much people can charge per hour so

00:31:33.904 --> 00:31:35.493
we don't have a higher.

00:31:35.493 --> 00:31:37.618
Well not people that are,
yeah, minimum, sorry.

00:31:37.618 --> 00:31:40.550
So we want people who are making more
money per hour because that's going to

00:31:40.550 --> 00:31:41.840
bump up the quality of the work.

00:31:41.840 --> 00:31:44.280
&gt;&gt; Right,
how much do you two charge per hour?

00:31:44.280 --> 00:31:48.900
We're both trying to charge about
150 an hour, and we're finding, so

00:31:48.900 --> 00:31:52.800
we've had about 300 people sign up so far,
and we asked them what their hourly rate

00:31:52.800 --> 00:31:57.270
is, and it's Usually from
about 70 to 200 per hour.

00:31:57.270 --> 00:32:00.130
&gt;&gt; The interesting thing is that it's not
so much how much the people are charging,

00:32:00.130 --> 00:32:02.480
it's more of the perceived value
on the other companies, right?

00:32:02.480 --> 00:32:05.750
So if the company's saying these
developers are worth $150 or

00:32:05.750 --> 00:32:10.370
$200 an hour, they are happy to pay that,
but you need to convince them that

00:32:10.370 --> 00:32:13.570
they're worth that money, regardless of
what the developers think they're worth.

00:32:13.570 --> 00:32:14.820
And how do you do that?

00:32:14.820 --> 00:32:17.900
How do you a convince a company that
your developers are worth $200 an hour

00:32:17.900 --> 00:32:20.615
if they can go somewhere for $25 an hour?

00:32:20.615 --> 00:32:22.334
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I think right now for

00:32:22.334 --> 00:32:26.764
the MVP what we're trying to say is we
are personally experts in these fields and

00:32:26.764 --> 00:32:30.624
we are making sure that the quality
of talent we have is up to that par.

00:32:30.624 --> 00:32:32.503
I think that we need to figure out
how we're going to scale that.

00:32:32.503 --> 00:32:36.037
&gt;&gt; I also think, in addition to
figuring out how to scale it,

00:32:36.037 --> 00:32:38.717
you need to figure out how you're going to
communicate it, like the website really

00:32:38.717 --> 00:32:41.957
has to give across the impression with
testimonials from well known customers or

00:32:41.957 --> 00:32:44.827
whatever that like,
these are the best people.

00:32:44.827 --> 00:32:49.464
In terms of setting the prices,
one thing we have consistently seen is

00:32:49.464 --> 00:32:53.641
when you ask a user to set their
price limit in the marketplace.

00:32:53.641 --> 00:32:57.642
If you give them guidance, if you say like
based on what you've said here's what we

00:32:57.642 --> 00:33:01.660
recommend or here's the range we
recommend, 85, 90% of users will do that.

00:33:01.660 --> 00:33:03.386
And so if you want to set,

00:33:03.386 --> 00:33:08.160
if you want to sort of move people
towards a specific range because you

00:33:08.160 --> 00:33:13.040
want people that are at that quality
level, you can probably do that.

00:33:13.040 --> 00:33:18.092
&gt;&gt; So we did that, our sign up form
says the average hourly rate is

00:33:18.092 --> 00:33:23.435
$70 per hour and it also says,
if you have your annual salary,

00:33:23.435 --> 00:33:26.761
that's about what your hourly rate is.

00:33:26.761 --> 00:33:28.800
So $100,000 per year
is about $50 per hour.

00:33:28.800 --> 00:33:34.300
&gt;&gt; When do you think you'll have
the first external developer live, right?

00:33:34.300 --> 00:33:36.430
So that's not the two of you.

00:33:36.430 --> 00:33:40.070
&gt;&gt; So we're working basically on
building out invoicing right now.

00:33:40.070 --> 00:33:43.140
And so that'll be the next week or
two probably.

00:33:43.140 --> 00:33:45.360
&gt;&gt; Do you need, like can you do it
more manually in the beginning?

00:33:45.360 --> 00:33:47.565
Just so
that you can see someone who's not you,

00:33:47.565 --> 00:33:50.490
because you obviously don't
have invoicing, right?

00:33:50.490 --> 00:33:53.460
&gt;&gt; So we've been doing this ourselves and
there's just a lot of moving parts.

00:33:53.460 --> 00:33:55.620
There's sending contracts back and forth.

00:33:55.620 --> 00:33:58.840
There's like setting up bank accounts,
there's getting companies to pay.

00:33:58.840 --> 00:34:01.960
And when we talk to the potential
contractors who want to be on

00:34:01.960 --> 00:34:04.460
the platform,
some of their biggest concerns are if

00:34:04.460 --> 00:34:07.850
I'm only working 10 hours per week how
much time does it take to ramp up?

00:34:07.850 --> 00:34:10.912
Do I need to talk with the customer and
how long does it take until I get paid?

00:34:10.912 --> 00:34:12.051
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
I would try and

00:34:12.051 --> 00:34:13.503
hack all of that in a way and

00:34:13.503 --> 00:34:18.360
do it behind the scenes manually right now
just to get the developers developing.

00:34:18.360 --> 00:34:21.160
Take all the pressure away from them, so
that they can just start interacting with

00:34:21.160 --> 00:34:24.232
the customer and if you need to
manually process it do that.

00:34:24.232 --> 00:34:24.990
But like it's so

00:34:24.990 --> 00:34:28.590
important that someone who's not you
starts doing this on your platform.

00:34:30.130 --> 00:34:33.330
&gt;&gt; And then when they do, for
the first ones especially,

00:34:33.330 --> 00:34:37.870
I would be super involved just
to learn what the issues are.

00:34:37.870 --> 00:34:41.150
It may be that people aren't sure
they're allowed to do this and

00:34:41.150 --> 00:34:43.550
they need help looking at their
current employment contracts.

00:34:43.550 --> 00:34:47.790
It may be that people need training to
know how to engage with a customer.

00:34:47.790 --> 00:34:49.550
And like how much they should communicate.

00:34:49.550 --> 00:34:53.720
There are all of these things,
many of which will surprise you and

00:34:53.720 --> 00:34:55.890
your guesses on many will be wrong.

00:34:55.890 --> 00:35:01.010
And the way you find this out is to just,
like for the first let’s say like 20 users

00:35:01.010 --> 00:35:03.730
like go sit with them or
at least go talk to them all the time.

00:35:03.730 --> 00:35:06.100
&gt;&gt; I would almost think about it
like become their agent, right?

00:35:06.100 --> 00:35:08.564
Like if they were a talent and
you are an agent for them and

00:35:08.564 --> 00:35:11.722
you take care of everything for
them, and answer all their questions.

00:35:11.722 --> 00:35:14.430
&gt;&gt; And then you're going to just
start building that into the product.

00:35:14.430 --> 00:35:18.579
But in our experience almost every
time when the people think they know,

00:35:18.579 --> 00:35:21.231
like where the break
points are going to be, or

00:35:21.231 --> 00:35:25.193
what they need to teach their users,
they're almost always wrong.

00:35:25.193 --> 00:35:26.374
But you find it out really quickly.

00:35:26.374 --> 00:35:31.148
It's like, you don't need a giant
sample set to start hearing the common

00:35:31.148 --> 00:35:35.050
concerns people have at
every stage in the process.

00:35:35.050 --> 00:35:37.580
And then you just knock
those off one at a time.

00:35:37.580 --> 00:35:41.880
And that is a way I think you
can differentiate from other

00:35:41.880 --> 00:35:43.700
coding talent marketplaces
that don't do this.

00:35:45.120 --> 00:35:48.850
And if you can figure out, like if you
can be the person that makes it so

00:35:48.850 --> 00:35:51.440
that people who want
a little extra income, but

00:35:51.440 --> 00:35:55.000
don't know how to go be a freelancer
learn, and if that becomes your

00:35:55.000 --> 00:35:59.760
reputation, that'll be a great way
to get the top talent in the field.

00:36:00.970 --> 00:36:03.948
&gt;&gt; One of the other, just an idea,
which you may or may not want to pursue.

00:36:03.948 --> 00:36:06.460
But if you want to be known
as the experts in the field,

00:36:06.460 --> 00:36:09.786
see if you can get any experts to
actually use your platform, right?

00:36:09.786 --> 00:36:13.534
Through your network or somewhere else or
get someone who's well known on

00:36:13.534 --> 00:36:16.920
a particular Niche area, and
have them sign up on your platform and

00:36:16.920 --> 00:36:19.770
do projects on your platform,
if they're willing to.

00:36:19.770 --> 00:36:20.685
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:36:20.685 --> 00:36:22.819
&gt;&gt; So
what's your biggest problem right now?

00:36:22.819 --> 00:36:25.753
&gt;&gt; Us personally?

00:36:25.753 --> 00:36:26.634
&gt;&gt; The company.

00:36:26.634 --> 00:36:31.151
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; In this stage it's often one and

00:36:31.151 --> 00:36:32.240
the same.

00:36:32.240 --> 00:36:33.000
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:36:33.000 --> 00:36:37.621
&gt;&gt; I think it's more like just
getting the machinery up.

00:36:37.621 --> 00:36:42.315
Like if we were to manually take payments,
my biggest concern would be that

00:36:42.315 --> 00:36:47.242
10 months down the line, I'm going to
have to manually calculate 1099s.

00:36:47.242 --> 00:36:49.380
&gt;&gt; But you're not going to
be 10 months down the line.

00:36:49.380 --> 00:36:51.073
You need to get to the next two months.

00:36:51.073 --> 00:36:51.663
Yeah.

00:36:51.663 --> 00:36:52.214
&gt;&gt; Right?

00:36:52.214 --> 00:36:53.397
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:36:53.397 --> 00:36:56.320
&gt;&gt; I would say to go along with that,
the reason we feel like we have to

00:36:56.320 --> 00:36:58.978
have some sort of payment thing
set up is because there's so

00:36:58.978 --> 00:37:00.943
many laws around how you can pay people,
and

00:37:00.943 --> 00:37:04.640
like kind of the logistics of all that and
paperwork that needs to be gone through.

00:37:04.640 --> 00:37:07.547
So I think you are right we need to do
those first couple ones to know what

00:37:07.547 --> 00:37:08.050
those are.

00:37:08.050 --> 00:37:11.066
But I think we're both like-
&gt;&gt; Is there not just some other startup

00:37:11.066 --> 00:37:13.692
that you can use that handles
invoicing really well?

00:37:13.692 --> 00:37:15.060
&gt;&gt; Well when you-
&gt;&gt; It is complicated, your-

00:37:15.060 --> 00:37:15.688
&gt;&gt; We're using-

00:37:15.688 --> 00:37:16.248
&gt;&gt; Or just like

00:37:16.248 --> 00:37:17.880
an accountant that can help you.

00:37:17.880 --> 00:37:19.710
&gt;&gt; Yeah, do you want to answer it?

00:37:19.710 --> 00:37:23.490
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so we're using Stripe Connect
which handles everything including

00:37:23.490 --> 00:37:24.560
like the 1099s.

00:37:24.560 --> 00:37:29.272
But there's no turn key invoicing
system built on top of that right now.

00:37:29.272 --> 00:37:30.560
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I get that.

00:37:30.560 --> 00:37:35.300
I really do get that but I still feel like
it's so, I get that feeling as a developer

00:37:35.300 --> 00:37:38.180
all the time where it's like, if only I
have this built, I would be ready to go.

00:37:38.180 --> 00:37:41.200
But it's so much more important to
actually find out if you can, like even if

00:37:41.200 --> 00:37:44.100
you have the platform built, there is
a chance that people won't start using it.

00:37:44.100 --> 00:37:47.180
And you'll only know that when
you do the nonscalable things.

00:37:47.180 --> 00:37:50.670
That's one of the things we drive home
almost every single office hour session,

00:37:50.670 --> 00:37:53.430
which is just do the nonscalable things,
do it manually.

00:37:53.430 --> 00:37:55.000
Don't worry about 10 months down the road.

00:37:55.000 --> 00:37:57.640
The 10 months down the road
it's a new company.

00:37:57.640 --> 00:38:00.020
It will look very
different than it is today.

00:38:00.020 --> 00:38:03.570
But today it's so
important to do the nonscalable things.

00:38:03.570 --> 00:38:05.960
&gt;&gt; And we're just talking about
a small handful of users.

00:38:05.960 --> 00:38:09.010
Like you don't have to bring on,
everyone's like well

00:38:09.010 --> 00:38:12.740
my problem is if I do this nonscalable
thing, 2000 users will sign up tomorrow.

00:38:12.740 --> 00:38:13.434
&gt;&gt; You should be so lucky.

00:38:13.434 --> 00:38:17.670
&gt;&gt; And I'll have to deal with them and
that is not what's going to happen.

00:38:17.670 --> 00:38:22.915
The, It's a slightly worn instinct to say

00:38:22.915 --> 00:38:27.380
that my biggest problem is that I haven't
built this one piece of the software yet.

00:38:27.380 --> 00:38:31.710
Not that my biggest problem is how
I'm going to get those 2000 users.

00:38:31.710 --> 00:38:35.620
&gt;&gt; So what we're focusing on
basically is the invoicing right now.

00:38:35.620 --> 00:38:37.650
It's not people logging in or
anything like that.

00:38:37.650 --> 00:38:41.691
It's just like being able to take a credit
card or contact details right now.

00:38:41.691 --> 00:38:45.635
&gt;&gt; Well again, if that's the one thing
you want to do, I would get that.

00:38:45.635 --> 00:38:50.877
[CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; My

00:38:50.877 --> 00:38:55.903
answer to that question about your biggest
problem, I'm not worried about the fact

00:38:55.903 --> 00:39:00.885
that you'll be able to get people that
need developers to build something.

00:39:00.885 --> 00:39:05.500
There's a lot of people that
have an idea for an app.

00:39:05.500 --> 00:39:10.380
The thing that I worry about is every
once, so often witnessing the idea of

00:39:10.380 --> 00:39:14.690
I'm going to get these great developer
to sort of build stuff for other people.

00:39:14.690 --> 00:39:20.950
And it has been so hard to actually do
that because the reasons the people

00:39:20.950 --> 00:39:25.530
have thought they're going to get the
developers, or the sort of assumptions and

00:39:25.530 --> 00:39:28.870
developers know how to do this
well have just been so wrong.

00:39:28.870 --> 00:39:30.687
And so you still like,

00:39:30.687 --> 00:39:35.850
you won't notice your own problems
because you've thought about this a lot.

00:39:35.850 --> 00:39:40.720
And I think like, you may find out that
invoicing doesn't even need to be built

00:39:40.720 --> 00:39:41.850
the way you think it needs to be built,

00:39:43.210 --> 00:39:46.510
because there's something fundamental
about the model that's going to change.

00:39:46.510 --> 00:39:50.800
But getting people you don't know or
you don't know well,

00:39:50.800 --> 00:39:56.890
to sort of build projects on a platform,
I think that's a really important step.

00:39:58.200 --> 00:40:02.250
&gt;&gt; I agree with that, I would just
add in that the idea came from

00:40:02.250 --> 00:40:04.665
a prior company where we didn't have-
&gt;&gt; That's great.

00:40:04.665 --> 00:40:07.154
&gt;&gt; The time to go hire people and
we started hiring part time people,

00:40:07.154 --> 00:40:09.480
people who were like friends of friends,
things like that.

00:40:09.480 --> 00:40:13.555
And so we started to do a lot
of firsthand research like that.

00:40:13.555 --> 00:40:14.426
&gt;&gt; No, I think that's great.

00:40:14.426 --> 00:40:16.000
That's actually super important.

00:40:16.000 --> 00:40:19.320
Because it means you have the pain and
you understand the pain.

00:40:19.320 --> 00:40:22.630
But it's still like, Sam and I are just
kind of driving to the same thing.

00:40:22.630 --> 00:40:23.750
Which is just it's so

00:40:23.750 --> 00:40:27.450
critical to get someone who has nothing
to do with your previous business or

00:40:27.450 --> 00:40:31.000
your current kind of core business
that wants to start using it.

00:40:31.000 --> 00:40:32.040
&gt;&gt; That makes sense.

00:40:32.040 --> 00:40:33.980
&gt;&gt; Any other issues you'd
like to talk about?

00:40:33.980 --> 00:40:34.580
Any other questions?

00:40:36.843 --> 00:40:37.738
&gt;&gt; I don't think so.

00:40:37.738 --> 00:40:40.278
I think that's our main thing that
we know we need to do right now.

00:40:40.278 --> 00:40:41.670
&gt;&gt; Great, that's super exciting.

00:40:41.670 --> 00:40:44.860
&gt;&gt; And how did you decide about
15% is the amount to charge?

00:40:46.330 --> 00:40:50.050
&gt;&gt; I think that's another thing we need to
test out but we feel like that's a number

00:40:50.050 --> 00:40:53.285
that's not crazy for
businesses to pay on top of something.

00:40:53.285 --> 00:40:58.580
Like my roommate's a recruiter and
she gets 30% off the top of all of their

00:40:58.580 --> 00:41:03.800
salaries, but it's also enough for us to
not have too many people to start with and

00:41:03.800 --> 00:41:05.330
be able to start testing things out.

00:41:05.330 --> 00:41:08.193
&gt;&gt; Is that a price that you're going
to charge on top of the hourly rate, or

00:41:08.193 --> 00:41:10.264
is that just going to be
blended in to the hourly rate?

00:41:10.264 --> 00:41:13.840
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so if somebody's $70 an hour,
we'll add $15 to that.

00:41:13.840 --> 00:41:15.530
Sorry, $100 an hour we'll add $15 to that.

00:41:15.530 --> 00:41:16.290
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:41:16.290 --> 00:41:18.710
&gt;&gt; And this is a good problem
to have in the future.

00:41:18.710 --> 00:41:22.464
What will prevent people from,
once they work with someone they like,

00:41:22.464 --> 00:41:25.298
just hiring them off the platform and
not paying you?

00:41:25.298 --> 00:41:28.476
&gt;&gt; I think that's another thing
that a lot of developers and

00:41:28.476 --> 00:41:30.405
designers have pointed out to us.

00:41:30.405 --> 00:41:34.354
And I think we want to make sure that the
tools inside of the platform are keeping

00:41:34.354 --> 00:41:35.265
people there, so

00:41:35.265 --> 00:41:39.390
it's so easy for them to get working, keep
work and they can do this on the side.

00:41:39.390 --> 00:41:42.650
So building internal tools that
allow people to work remotely and

00:41:42.650 --> 00:41:43.830
us, contract workers?

00:41:43.830 --> 00:41:44.670
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Will be another thing.

00:41:44.670 --> 00:41:47.501
&gt;&gt; That's another thing that works
well about the short term projects.

00:41:47.501 --> 00:41:48.101
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:41:48.101 --> 00:41:48.866
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:41:48.866 --> 00:41:49.772
Great, thank you very much.

00:41:49.772 --> 00:41:50.297
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:41:50.297 --> 00:41:54.850
&gt;&gt; Thank you.
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:41:54.850 --> 00:41:55.430
&gt;&gt; Nice to meet you.

00:41:55.430 --> 00:41:55.938
&gt;&gt; Thank you.
&gt;&gt; You too.

00:41:55.938 --> 00:41:56.705
Yeah.

00:41:56.705 --> 00:42:00.360
&gt;&gt; So format wise, that was an experiment.

00:42:00.360 --> 00:42:02.680
Is that something you'd like
us to more of in the class?

00:42:02.680 --> 00:42:03.610
Was that useful?

00:42:03.610 --> 00:42:06.520
More regular lectures, yes, yes?

00:42:06.520 --> 00:42:07.250
Some middle, okay.

00:42:07.250 --> 00:42:08.915
We'll do some more about them.

00:42:08.915 --> 00:42:09.785
Great, thank you very much.

00:42:09.785 --> 00:42:11.331
Thank you, Eric.

00:42:11.331 --> 00:42:12.055
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:42:12.055 --> 00:42:12.905
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

